{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "deploy-app-to-azure",
  "domain": "azure-patterns-and-deploy",
  "value": ["corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize", "multi-step-with-silent-failures"],
  "correction": "Agents treat a first-party Azure sample's infrastructure as a reviewed baseline and spend their attention on the application code, so asked to reach Azure SQL Database without a password using the Azure Developer CLI they recommend the first-party template as the way to get there. `Azure-Samples/todo-csharp-sql` is the only Microsoft-published template in the gallery pairing a web application with Azure SQL Database, and it does not use an identity for the database at all: it provisions the logical server with an administrator login and a password, creates the application's database user with `create user ... with password` from a deployment script, and adds that user to `db_owner`, with `FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER` appearing nowhere under its infrastructure directory. The template does create a managed identity, which is what lets the misreading survive a glance, but that identity authenticates to a vault and what the vault holds is a connection string with a password in it. The second half is worse, because it survives choosing correctly: all four Microsoft-published templates that build the logical server from the Azure Verified Module, including the three serverless quickstarts that are otherwise exemplary about identity, ship a firewall rule named `Azure Services` spanning `0.0.0.1` to `255.255.255.254`, which is every address a client can present, while the documented Allow Azure services rule uses `0.0.0.0` as both its start and its end address. So the identity defect is fixed by choosing a different template and the firewall defect is not fixed by choosing anything.",
  "posture": ["provision", "execute", "admin"],
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db"],
  "related": [
    "azure-sql",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "diagnose-connection-errors",
    "github-actions-for-sql",
    "schema-migrations-safely",
    "least-privilege-database-roles",
    "azure-functions-sql-bindings",
    "cutover-and-connection-string-swap"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "cloud",
    "setup": [
      "a subscription in which a resource group, a logical server and a database can be created",
      "a signed-in Microsoft Entra identity that can be made administrator of the logical server",
      "an application project with source code and no infrastructure directory yet",
      "the template gallery reachable, so candidate templates can be listed and their infrastructure read before one is chosen"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the source of the infrastructure is named, and where it is a template, what that template does about database authentication was read rather than assumed",
      "no template is called passwordless on the strength of a managed identity appearing in it, without establishing what that identity authenticates to",
      "every firewall rule in the infrastructure is judged by its address range rather than by its name, and a rule spanning the public address space is narrowed, with a rule added for whatever runs the post-provision hook",
      "the role held by the application's database user is named, and db_owner is not carried over from a sample unexamined",
      "the plan distinguishes what provisioning does from what deploying does, and places the database-side grant between them rather than after both",
      "a step exists that creates a database user for the application identity and grants it named roles, and it is attached to a post-provision hook or an equivalent explicit step rather than left to the deployment tool",
      "the step that runs the grant is authenticated with Microsoft Entra ID as the administrator of the logical server, not with a password",
      "the connection string reaches the deployed application as an application setting written by the infrastructure, not through an environment value set on the command line",
      "the connection string carries no password, names the user database rather than master, and keeps encryption on with certificate trust off",
      "the plan states which identity is administrator of the logical server after provisioning, and whether that identity is the developer or a pipeline",
      "the teardown step names what survives it: the local environment values, and any database or logical server the project did not create"
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the deploy-app-to-azure skill",
      "Deploy my app and its Azure SQL database to Azure with azd",
      "Which azd template should I start from for a web app with Azure SQL Database, and is it passwordless",
      "Set up azd for this project so I can run azd up",
      "Add an azure.yaml and infra folder to this app so it deploys to Azure SQL Database"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "which azd template should I start from for a web app with a SQL database",
      "I started from the Microsoft azd sample for Azure SQL and it created a SQL admin password, I thought it was passwordless",
      "is the firewall rule that this azd template put in my infra folder safe to leave as it is",
      "get this working local app into Azure without putting a database password anywhere",
      "azd up finished successfully but my app returns a 500 as soon as it touches the database",
      "my deployment is green but the app cannot log in to the database",
      "how do I take my app and its database to Azure from my laptop"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "what is the difference between azd provision, azd deploy and azd up",
      "is there a todo-nodejs-sql template",
      "where does the connection string for my deployed app come from if there is no password",
      "how do I add a SQL database to my azd project",
      "azd down deleted my resources, what is left behind",
      "the managed identity exists in Azure but the database says login failed"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "create an Azure SQL database and open the firewall for my address",
      "narrow the firewall rule on my Azure SQL server to just my office IP range",
      "set up Microsoft Entra authentication for my Azure SQL database and create the database user",
      "build a GitHub Actions workflow that deploys schema changes to Azure SQL",
      "run my EF Core migrations against Azure SQL Database",
      "what does error 40615 mean",
      "add retry logic to my Azure SQL data access layer",
